      "## Preserve; do not improve the chart after the fact",
      "Once litigation or an investigation is anticipated, routine destruction may need to stop. Never delete an embarrassing message, rewrite a note, insert material as if it were contemporaneous, or create a cleaner “legal copy.” If clarification is needed, use a dated late entry or amendment that preserves the original according to policy.",
      "Metadata, audit logs, drafts, emails, texts, portal messages, billing records, and backups may be records or discoverable material even when they are not part of the designated clinical record set. Counselors should write all professional communications with accuracy and respect.",
      "## Testimony and role boundaries",
      "A treating counselor can provide factual testimony or records yet still be unqualified or ethically poorly positioned to offer a forensic opinion about custody, criminal responsibility, disability, or ultimate legal issues. Before testifying:",
      "- Clarify whether testimony is fact, treatment opinion, expert opinion, or forensic evaluation.\n- Review competence, conflicts, informed consent, privilege, and potential harm.\n- Avoid advocacy that distorts clinical facts.\n- Do not opine beyond the data, methods, role, or expertise.\n- Prepare through lawful consultation, not by coaching the client.",
