      "## Avoiding abandonment",
      "Abandonment is not the same as every termination or interruption. Counselors may end or transfer services for appropriate reasons, but they should provide reasonable notice and support when possible, address foreseeable risk, offer referrals or alternatives, and facilitate continuity.",
      "Consider:",
      "- Client clinical needs and urgency\n- Length and intensity of the relationship\n- Availability of comparable care\n- Geographic, financial, language, disability, and cultural barriers\n- The counselor's capacity and legal authority\n- The reason and timing for ending services\n- What interim support is reasonably possible",
      "Do not promise indefinite access. Do not disappear because a client owes money, criticizes the counselor, travels, or requires a difficult accommodation. Apply informed-consent terms and ethical transition procedures.",
      "## Counselor incapacity or death",
      "A solo counselor should establish a professional will or equivalent continuity arrangement consistent with local law and ethics. It can identify:",
      "- Authorized records custodian and scope of access\n- How active clients will be notified without unnecessary disclosure\n- Who will manage urgent messages and records requests\n- How referrals, billing, and refunds are handled\n- Where credentials, contracts, and vendor instructions are stored securely\n- How records remain protected, retained, accessed, and disposed of\n- How the plan is activated, reviewed, and tested",
